    "Long-term survival after surgery for Tetralogy of Fallot (TOF) continues to improve, but residual hemodynamic and electrophysiological abnormalities remain common in adulthood. Patients with repaired TOF face an increased risk of arrhythmias, exercise intolerance, heart failure, and death beginning in early adulthood. The most common hemodynamic sequela of TOF repair is pulmonary regurgitation (PR). Surgery for PR can improve hemodynamics and reduce risks, though surgical intervention timing remains uncertain. Primary prevention ICDs may be considered for those with standard qualifying criteria or specific risk factors. Surgical and therapeutic interventions are recommended based on ventricular size, function, and risk of sudden cardiac death (SCD).": "",
